.TH WAIT 2
.SH NAME
wait, await, exits, exit, getpid \- wait for a process to end, end a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "wait :: proc(pid: u64) -> i64"
.B "await :: proc(pid: u64, buf: []u8) -> i64"
.B "exits :: proc(status: string) -> !"
.B "exit :: proc(status: u64) -> !"
.B "getpid :: proc() -> u64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Exits
ends the calling process with
.IR status ,
its exit status: empty for success, a few words otherwise.
.I Exit
ends it instead with a number, which
.I wait
answers as
.IR status 's
low 32 bits and
.I await
writes as a decimal word if it is not zero.
Neither call returns.
.PP
.I Wait
collects one child of the caller, by
.IR pid ,
and answers its exit status: the low bits of what it gave
.I exit
or
.IR exits ,
a note's name turned into
.B EINTR
if a note ended it without a handler, or
.B EIO
if a fault ended it.
Only a child of the caller may be named; a
.I pid
that belongs to someone else, or to nobody, answers
.BR ECHILD .
Collecting a child destroys its record: a second
.I wait
on the same
.I pid
also answers
.BR ECHILD .
A child that has not ended within the kernel's patience answers
.BR EAGAIN ,
and the caller may ask again.
.PP
.I Await
is Plan 9's call of the same name: it collects one child and writes
.RI \(lq pid
.RI [ word ]\(rq
into
.IR buf ,
where
.I word
is the child's exit status if it said anything, or the text of the note
that ended it, and answers the length written.
A
.I pid
of zero collects any child of the caller that has ended, waiting for one
to if none has yet.
.PP
.I Getpid
answers the calling process's own pid, the name it is known by under
.BR /proc .
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/spawn.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR notify (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Wait
and
.I await
answer a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
.I Getpid
always succeeds.
